1234: 4452:Every read here runs outside a transaction, in the order of the unit's checks (section 6.5): the stored grade (IdempotentCreate on the natural key `ResponseId`, `HvRequestId`, kind `Model`, policy `Conflict`, read-only, so a retry after the request completed or the response was rejected still returns the stored grade), the response, each finding's `claimId`, the HV request, the response's status, the HV request's status, the item, and the eight receipt, reviewer, and runtime-identity checks of section 6.3 over the receipt files and the reviewer's own session-log turn. A failed check is answered with `details.check`; the reviewer makes its submission equal its verdict entry and its turn, or reruns the HV on the configured grader. EvaluateStalenessCommand then persists `Stale` when a dependency of the response or its package changed, so the trust decision in the SerializableWrite never trusts it (a `Stale` response stays gradable, and the decision leaves it `Stale`); when that dispatch's own write exhausts its four attempts, the grade answers 409 `conflict` with `retryable: true` and saves nothing. The response's `StatusVersion` and the HV request's state that the SerializableWrite re-checks are the ones read after that dispatch.
1235: 5160:### 7.4 Use case: Close every request turn before stopping (plan UC-3) [FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005, -006]
1236: 5162:UC-3 Close every request turn before stopping. Actors: model, host Stop hook. Basic flow: model records actions/decisions (incl. via `designDecisions`) and closes each turn (a `completed` or `failed` close requires model evidence, Test-ModelEvidence); Stop hook (process EvaluateStopGate) finds all closed with evidence and passes, clearing the ledger. Alternate: a turn is open (including one whose close the plugin refused for missing model evidence) or its most recent close was refused by the server's completion gate, the Stop hook blocks with the reason and the accepted remedy (Get-CloseRemedy); a turn closed without evidence by another surface is only warned and never closes turns itself. Alternate: the host session failed closed under D6 (`unsupportedSince`) while a turn was open; the Stop hook blocks with the reduced remedy (only the missing model evidence, the missing `requestClass` from S8/S9, and the close call; the terminal reviews are judged at the drain), the model's evidence and close for that turn are captured in the plugin failsafe (reviews are refused and recorded after the drain through the original pair), the next Stop warns instead of blocking, and a later SessionStart drains the captured records with the turn's original pair through the trusted recovery flow (a post-compact SessionStart of the still-live host session drains none of them). Alternate: a model close held in the failsafe by a transport outage, or the package-failure `canceled` close of a stateless or pilot invocation (section 7.15) held there, is warned as degraded persistence (naming the package failure for the `canceled` close, which never needs model evidence) and never blocks; its ledger entry stays until the drain confirms the close. Alternate: a contended turn-open lock or a final mint refusal at prompt time left no turn, and the Stop hook warns naming the prompt. Alternate: the failsafe quarantine holds a record of an open turn, and the Stop hook warns and never blocks (section 3.1, "Failsafe drain"). A missing agent-context response submission (section 7.12) is not a Stop-gate item. Realizes FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005, -006; updated FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-005, FR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001.
1237: 5230:UC-7 Record a hostile validation review with receipts. Actors: hostile reviewer (Codex CLI, gpt-6-astra, max), coordinator agent. Basic flow: at each HV point of "Execution organization" once its gates are green (D10), the coordinator writes the HV brief and launches the Codex reviewer; the runner records the request and response jsonl; the reviewer writes its markdown and JSON receipts and stores its entire result on its own session-log turn; from the Phase 4 live-check deploy on, the coordinator records `hostileReview` from the receipt (accuracy/completeness mapped, receipt paths set) and backfills the Phase 4 context verdicts by original pair before the phase-wide Phase 4 HV; for every HV point before that deploy, it records the verdict on the work package's coordinating turn (dialog and designDecisions with the receipt paths) and with `RecordValidationResultAsync`. Alternate: DISAGREE with passing scores recorded as Other with notes; missing receipt paths rejected; a retryable answer to the review write is reported with its code and `retryable: true`, exit 1, never queued, and the coordinator records the review again after the drain (as in UC-6, section 5.2); `hvVerdict` sent together with an explicit `status` is refused with 400 `validation_error` naming `status`; a run whose live model or effort is not gpt-6-astra/max is not accepted as HV (an HV-protocol rule: the coordinator checks the reviewer's recorded model and effort in its receipts before recording any verdict, so process ConvertHvReceiptToReview and the plugin do not check it); from the Phase 4 live-check deploy on, Code or Docs turns of a work package that completed before its HV carry `Other` "pending HV <gate>" and receive the verdict through `recordReview`, each addressed by its original (`sessionId`, `requestId`) pair (FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-002-AC002). The reviewer's brief is a user prompt and opens its own turn, because the prompt hook classifies a background agent only by the host's subagent markers, never by prompt text (section 5.4; this replaces FR-MCP-TRIAGEPLUGIN-001's text-detected background-brief reuse); the HostileReview subsystem (FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006, its `xhigh` worker, and its AGREE, DISAGREE, or UNKNOWN verdicts) is separate: its results are not HV receipts for this use case and are not judged by its `max` rule (section 5.4; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 is unchanged). Realizes FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-002, -005 (-005 updated with that separation); updated FR-MCP-TRIAGEPLUGIN-001.
